---
title: logrotate
order: 2
description: logrotate config, manual rotation, compression
tags: [logrotate, log-rotation, compression, logs]
---

logrotate manages log file rotation, compression, and deletion to prevent unbounded growth.

@text
Basic Usage

@code bash
# Run logrotate manually (dry run / debug)
sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf

# Force rotation (ignore time conditions)
sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf

# Rotate a specific config file only
sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/syslog-ng

# Verbose output
sudo logrotate -v /etc/logrotate.conf

# State file (tracks last rotation times)
cat /var/lib/logrotate/logrotate.status

@text
Automatic Rotation

On OpenRC/Artix, logrotate is typically run by a cron job (not a systemd timer).

@code bash
# Check if cron job exists
cat /etc/cron.daily/logrotate
# or
ls /etc/cron.daily/ | grep logrotate

# Typical cron entry (/etc/cron.daily/logrotate):
# #!/bin/sh
# /usr/sbin/logrotate /etc/logrotate.conf

@text
Configuration — /etc/logrotate.conf

@code bash
# /etc/logrotate.conf — main config with global defaults:
#
# weekly              — rotate weekly (daily, weekly, monthly, yearly)
# rotate 4            — keep 4 rotated files
# create              — create new empty file after rotation
# dateext             — use date in rotated filename (instead of .1, .2)
# compress            — gzip rotated files
# include /etc/logrotate.d   — include per-app configs

@text
Per-Application Config — /etc/logrotate.d/

@code bash
# Example: /etc/logrotate.d/syslog-ng
# /var/log/messages /var/log/auth.log /var/log/kern.log {
#     rotate 12
#     monthly
#     compress
#     delaycompress
#     missingok
#     notifempty
#     postrotate
#         rc-service syslog-ng reload > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
#     endscript
# }

# Example: custom application log
# /var/log/myapp/*.log {
#     daily
#     rotate 7
#     compress
#     delaycompress
#     missingok
#     notifempty
#     create 0640 myuser mygroup
#     sharedscripts
#     postrotate
#         rc-service myapp reload > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
#     endscript
# }

@text
Key Directives

@code bash
# Rotation frequency:
# daily, weekly, monthly, yearly
# size 100M            — rotate when file exceeds size
# minsize 10M          — rotate at frequency but only if >= size
# maxsize 500M         — rotate early if file exceeds this size

# Retention:
# rotate 7             — keep 7 old copies
# maxage 30            — delete rotated files older than N days

# Compression:
# compress             — compress rotated files (gzip)
# delaycompress        — delay compression by one rotation cycle
# compresscmd /usr/bin/zstd   — use a different compressor
# compressoptions "-T0"       — compressor options
# uncompresscmd /usr/bin/unzstd

# File creation:
# create 0640 root adm — create new log with these permissions
# nocreate             — don't create new file
# copytruncate         — copy then truncate (for apps that hold fd open)
# copy                 — copy but don't truncate

# Notifications:
# notifempty           — skip rotation if file is empty
# missingok            — don't error if log file is missing
# ifempty              — rotate even if empty (default)

# Scripts:
# prerotate/endscript  — run before rotation
# postrotate/endscript — run after rotation
# sharedscripts        — run scripts once (not per matched file)
# firstaction/endscript — run before all rotations
# lastaction/endscript  — run after all rotations

@text
Troubleshooting

@code bash
# Debug — show what would happen without doing it
sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/syslog-ng

# Force a specific file to rotate
sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/syslog-ng

# Reset state (force all files to rotate next run)
sudo rm /var/lib/logrotate/logrotate.status

# Check permissions on log files
ls -la /var/log/messages
namei -l /var/log/messages
